    <Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Knowledge',href:'/knowledge/'},{label:'Stability & packaging'}]} />
    <h1>Cosmetic peptide stability and packaging</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Most raw-material degradation happens before the formula is made. Packaging and storage are not logistics details — they are the first stability control.</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/cosmetic-peptide-raw-materials-framework/">cosmetic peptide raw materials pillar</a>.</p>

    <h2>Three exposure routes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Exposure</th><th>Effect</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Light</td><td>Photodegradation; colour drift</td><td>Amber/opaque containers; dark storage</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Oxidation (esp. glutathione); colour change</td><td>Minimal headspace; nitrogen flush; sealed barrier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moisture</td><td>Hydrolysis; caking; microbial risk</td><td>Desiccant; sealed primary pack; low-humidity handling</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Container choice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Amber glass:</strong> good light barrier; check stopper compatibility with the material.</li>
      <li><strong>Opaque HDPE:</strong> practical for bulk; confirm extractables and moisture barrier.</li>
      <li><strong>Foil barrier bags:</strong> for bulk powder; good moisture and light protection if sealed.</li>
      <li><strong>Headspace:</strong> minimise for oxygen-sensitive materials; consider nitrogen.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Storage conditions</h2>
    <p>Cool, dry, dark, sealed. Avoid frost-free freezers with temperature cycling; avoid storage near oxidisers or metal surfaces. For glutathione in particular, keep away from transition metals.</p>

    <h2>In-use stability</h2>
    <p>Once a container is opened, the clock starts. Record the opening date, reseal promptly, and use within a defined period. For a raw material used over months, split into smaller containers rather than repeatedly opening the bulk pack.</p>

    <h2>What to ask the supplier</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Recommended storage temperature and shelf life, with supporting data.</li>
      <li>Container and closure specification; extractables statement.</li>
      <li>Light and oxygen protection measures used in packing.</li>
      <li>Retest date and batch number on the label.</li>
    </ol>


    <h2>Accelerated versus real-time</h2>
    <p>Accelerated conditions are a screening tool, not a shelf-life claim. A material that darkens at elevated temperature tells you which failure mode to watch; it does not tell you how long the product lasts at 25 °C. Ask for real-time data when a shelf-life claim matters, and treat accelerated results as a ranking of risks.</p>

    <h2>Container qualification</h2>
    <p>Before committing to a container, check three things: moisture ingress over the intended storage period, light transmission at the relevant wavelengths, and extractables from the closure. A container that passes the first two but leaches into the product is not a container; it is an ingredient.</p>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Packaging is the first stability study and the cheapest one. Most raw-material degradation is decided before the formula is opened.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>This is formulation research guidance; no cosmetic efficacy claim is made. Finished-product safety and claims are the formulator responsibility.</p>


    <h2>A storage decision tree</h2>
    <p>Ask three questions in order: is the material oxygen-sensitive (glutathione) or light-sensitive (copper complexes)? Will the container be opened repeatedly? What is the expected time between receipt and use? The answers determine whether you need nitrogen, a foil barrier, single-use aliquots or all three. Packaging that ignores these questions is chosen by price, not by stability.</p>
    <p>Record the opening date on every container. An in-use period without a start date is not a control; it is a guess.</p>

